  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 126 PM MDT Tue Oct 27 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA baggy upper level trough remains in place across the NW US and \nNorthern Rockies with one of multiples embedded waves lifting out \ninto the Canadian Prairies today while another cuts east from \nsouthern ID across WY tonight. Energy dropping south along the BC \ncoast consolidates the trough to a closed low offshore of the Pacific \nNW by Friday, which eventually opens and shifts inland this weekend. \n\nLingering afternoon light precipitation near the Hi-line associated \nwith the energy lifting north into AB/SK will end by this evening. \nGusty winds that developed today behind the departing wave will diminish\nfor many areas this evening though breezy winds may persist through\ntonight along areas immediately adjacent to the Rocky Mtn Front \nand central MT mountains. A cooler airmass developing across AB \ntonight may expand as far south as areas near the international \nBorder by early Wednesday morning, bringing some risk for lower \nclouds/patchy fog there. The cooler airmass slides south across \nthe N-central MT plains later Wednesday through Thursday, cooling \ndaytime temperatures back to below seasonal averages with less \nnoticeable cooling reaching SW MT. \n\nWhile most of the forecast area is expected to see overall dry \nconditions through Thursday, a steady stream of Pacific moisture \nembedded within the westerly flow will support orographically \nenhanced showers over the continental divide with more persistent \nperiods of snow most likely for areas along the divide north of \nMT-200. A few isolated rain showers may track further east across\nnorth-central and SW MT, mainly in the afternoon and early \nevening periods today and again tomorrow as less stable conditions\noffset the effects of downslope drying in westerly flow. \n\nThe upper low offshore of the Pacific NW late this week splits while \nmoving onshore this weekend in tandem with a southern stream wave \nand Pacific moisture streaming into CA and the Great Basin. \nSoutherly flow developing Friday night and Saturday ahead of the \ntrough will bring some warming as well as increasing clouds with \nprecipitation chances increasing from south to north late Saturday. \nThe bulk of the precipitation looks to occur later Saturday night \nthrough Sunday with the passage of the trough axis and a slow moving \nPacific cold front. Snow with this system will initially be limited \nto higher elevations with lowering snow levels allowing for a mix\nor transition to snow at lower elevations Sunday. A drier period \nlooks to follow early next week as ridging builds in behind the \nweekend trough. Hoenisch \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nSnow levels along northern portions of the continental Divide (N of \nMT-200) fluctuate around 5000 ft through Thursday. Periods of snow \nwith some minor (<1\"/12hr) accumulation may occur at Marias Pass, \nparticularly Wednesday night, but accumulations of 2\" or more are \nprimarily expected to be limited to elevations >6000 ft in Glacier \nNP. \n\nInteractions between the splitting northern stream trough and \nsouthern stream moisture/energy this weekend lead to model differences\nand uncertainty in the more specific details of timing and precipitation\namounts/type still at this time range. Snow levels may initially \nbe above 7000 ft Saturday night but are likely to lower at least \nto pass levels on Sunday. Probabilities for >4\" snowfall amounts \nthis weekend are highest (50-80%) above 7000 ft in the Little \nBelt, Bridger, Gallatin and Madison Ranges with a 20-40% probability\nfor 2\" or higher amounts across most central and SW MT mountain \npasses. Hoenisch",
